WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.968
The basic authorization question is: who can do what to which resource under what conditions?

2
00:00:05.968 --> 00:00:08.990
The subject is the identity requesting access.

3
00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.861
The action is the operation being attempted.

4
00:00:11.861 --> 00:00:14.656
The resource is the object being protected.

5
00:00:14.656 --> 00:00:28.632
Context includes surrounding facts that may affect the decision, such as ownership, tenant membership, group membership, data sensitivity, environment, device posture, time, location concepts, or workflow state.

6
00:00:28.632 --> 00:00:32.258
Subjects and identities need to be represented clearly.

7
00:00:32.258 --> 00:00:37.999
A human user may act through a browser session, mobile client, API token, or delegated app.

8
00:00:37.999 --> 00:00:40.870
A workload may act through a service account.

9
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:44.118
An automation job may act without a person present.

10
00:00:44.118 --> 00:00:55.223
Authorization logic should know which subject is making the request and whether the subject is acting directly, through delegation, or as part of a service-to-service flow.

11
00:00:55.223 --> 00:01:00.512
Resources and actions should be specific enough to support meaningful decisions.

12
00:01:00.512 --> 00:01:05.573
A generic permission such as manage everything is easy to administer but risky.

13
00:01:05.573 --> 00:01:15.318
More precise actions, such as view invoice, update invoice status, export tenant report, or rotate integration credential, give teams better control.

14
00:01:15.318 --> 00:01:22.571
Resource identity matters too: being able to view one record does not imply access to every record of the same type.

15
00:01:22.571 --> 00:01:28.539
Authorization decisions should be explicit, reviewable, and close to the sensitive action.

16
00:01:28.539 --> 00:01:36.622
A check performed far away from the operation can be forgotten, bypassed by another code path, or made invalid by later changes.

17
00:01:36.622 --> 00:01:47.727
Clear subject, resource, action, and context modeling gives developers and reviewers a shared language for access control instead of relying on assumptions hidden in code.
